WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.540
Speaker 1: All right, this next story is a
very big deal.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:10.620
The Jan. six Trump Riot Committee, the committee
investigating the Jan. six, 2021, Trump riots,

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.429
is quite frankly doing a very good job of
something that is very difficult.

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:19.460
There are so many different aspects to the
Trump riots.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:25.560
What was Trump up to in the time leading up
to what was going on within law enforcement?

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.320
What were Trump's kids doing?

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.130
What were members of the House doing?

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.750
What were members of the Senate doing?

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:36.230
Who knew what, when, etc. records that need
to be obtained?

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.190
All these different things.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:47.310
The last two weeks have generated new evidence
for the committee because Donald Trump's reaction

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.780
to the committee's actions now appear to be
witness tampering.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:58.960
And in fact, Congressman Pete Aguilar said
on CNN yesterday Trump is tampering with witnesses,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.749
which is again scaling up the criminality
potential of what Donald Trump has been up

00:01:04.749 --> 00:01:05.749
to.

00:01:05.749 --> 00:01:12.440
So we have a CNN report about this January
6th committee member says Trump absolutely

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.170
tampering with witnesses as he continues to
dangle pardons.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.509
Let's take a look at this video clip here.

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:25.749
Speaker 4: That lawyer represents other Jan.
six defendants and said that Rhodes and others

00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:27.499
would welcome a Trump pardon.

00:01:27.499 --> 00:01:31.840
Is Trump tampering with witnesses when he's
talking about pardons, when he's dangling

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.789
them in front of one six defendants?

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:38.399
Speaker 3: Absolutely.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:43.079
And I think the question is more for my colleagues
on the other side of the aisle, you know,

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:44.619
where where are they?

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.520
Do they support this?

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.029
When is enough enough?

00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:50.470
When a mob is chanting, Hang Mike Pence?

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.810
It wasn't enough.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:57.200
When the former president asked Brad Raffensperger
to find him 11000 votes frozen enough.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.399
Right now, he's dangling a pardons if he gets
back in office for individuals.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:05.909
Will that be enough or will there be more
collective amnesia?

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:09.100
You know, I just don't know where the floor
is these days on that.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.269
Where is the
Speaker 1: floor and what is the bottom or

00:02:11.269 --> 00:02:15.299
whatever it is that you want to describe it
and what's being referred to here.

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:22.730
And what's funny is this isn't even the first
time that Donald Trump is dangling pardons

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.790
in a way that could be seen as witness tampering.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:33.819
Remember that back with during the Russia
probe, where you had Paul Manafort, Carter

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:41.069
Page, Roger Stone, all these different people
being investigated and interviewed, and many

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:46.349
subsequently charged for lying to the FBI
or different things.

00:02:46.349 --> 00:02:48.650
Donald Trump did the exact same thing.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.209
He talked about those who are staying strong.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:57.900
And he talked in a circuitous sort of roundabout
way about the possibility of pardons.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.620
It could have been argued at that point, and
many legal experts did argue that's a form

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.689
of witness tampering.

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:07.439
You were signaling to these individuals if
you don't cooperate.

00:03:07.439 --> 00:03:10.940
I may pardon you in the future if you don't
cooperate.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.269
There may be good things coming, and if you
do cooperate, you're going to be on the list

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:19.980
of bad boys, so to speak, and you're no longer
going to be in my good graces or whatever

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:20.980
the case may be.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:26.879
Here, Donald Trump is doing a very similar
thing with the Trump rioters themselves.

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:31.510
It's different because there's a really good
chance Trump is never going to pardon any

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.510
of them.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.670
And this is sort of like multistep.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:40.159
It's not at all clear that Donald Trump will
be the Republican nominee in 2024.

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:43.680
If he is the Republican nominee, it's not
at all clear that Donald Trump will win the

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.569
presidential election in 2024 if Trump wins
the 2024 presidential presidential election,

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:53.980
there is no guarantee that he will actually
pardon any of these people.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:04:00.939
And so not only is it another attempt to potentially
influence the behavior of the writers and

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:03.939
of those, and this is an important addition.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:08.819
It's not only about influencing the behavior
of the writers themselves in their cases,

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:14.189
but also of Republicans, more so in the House,
but in theory in the House and Senate and

00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:18.700
other people around Trump in general about
cooperation with the committee or the Trump

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:19.700
right.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.790
It's a bigger signal.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:25.010
But it's not even clear that Trump really
intends to follow through on this.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.010
And you can.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:30.640
You can make the argument, as Pete Aguilar
does on CNN, that it is a form of witness

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.640
tampering.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:36.100
Now, the obvious question is, well, so should
we expect a criminal referral to the Department

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.260
of Justice?

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.360
And it is not because Donald Trump will go
to prison.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.100
I maintain Trump is not going to prison.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.070
But at what point do we say?

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:53.260
The facts just point to there should be a
criminal referral from the Jan. six committee

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.840
to the Department of Justice and then put
it on the Department of Justice to decide

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.970
we will or won't investigate, we will or won't
charge, put it on Merrick Garland to actually

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.390
sort of be pushed into having to do something
when it comes to this.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.170
When is enough enough?

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:13.790
And consider with Hillary Clinton vis-a-vis
Benghazi.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:19.430
Endless hearings, special investigation hearing
report all these different things.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.780
Demanding charges demanding Hillary Clinton
be locked up.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.060
Absent any charges.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.710
Ignoring their stated principle of the value
of due process and constitutionality.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.680
And the rule of law and law and order.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.270
And all these things.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.590
There wasn't even anything there and they
were saying Lock up Hillary Clinton.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:44.250
It seems at minimum here there is more than
enough evidence and factual basis for a criminal

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.870
referral to the Department of Justice.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.880
And then let's see how the Department of Justice
deals with it.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:56.570
I don't expect this to stop, and we continue
to follow the now more than 700 cases 700

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:02.360
defendants in the Trump riots, now 13, almost
13 months to the day beyond that date.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:07.400
The sentences are starting to come in and
we will see where it ends up, but without

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.140
a doubt, this is arguably another instance
of witness tampering.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:17.230
And if you're Trump, if there were no repercussions
to the earlier witness tampering with the

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:18.890
Russia probe.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:22.100
Why would you behave any differently now,
of course you wouldn't, and that's part of

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.260
this entire thing.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:27.440
We'll have that clip of Congressman Pete Aguilar
on our Instagram.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.150
You can find that at David Pakman show, we're
going to take a quick break.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:36.470
And we've got a really interesting interview
coming up not about the one percent, but about

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.650
the nine point nine percent.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:39.650
What does that mean?

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.790
It's a very interesting group, culturally
and economically.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:07:52.740
And we're going to talk about that after this
short break.

